covery to continue and be  relatable to humans so with that I'll  conclude my presentation and send a huge  thank you to again lab roots and the  organizers of this event and to all of  my colleagues within the NCI and our  comparative Oncology  program and all of the various partners  collaborators and champions that we've  had both within NCI and IH and within  the drug development community as a  whole and a special thank you to our  students and member institutions and all  of the support staff the families of the  dog patients in your role on our trials  and for everyone's hard work to help  realize where the value of our study of  national Korean cancers and dogs can  really benefit both us and and now so  I'll thank you for your attention and be  happy to follow up with any questions  via email after the presentation  concludes thank you thank you dr.  LeBlanc for that informative  presentation I would also like to think  levers for making today's educational  webcast possible before we go I want to  let everyone know that this webcast will  be available for on-demand viewing  through May of 2019 and there's a final  reminder once again our speaker will  follow up with any questions you've  submitted via email that's all for now  thank you for joining us and we will see  you next time goodbye
 

